  "_about": "Wars fought away from the main fronts, each to its own plan. A theatre is a list of regions. A computer-run nation with a plan here runs every corps it has standing in those regions by that plan, and the rest of its army by its main plan in plan-historical.json: neither takes orders meant for the other, and battles on a theatre's ground roll their own dice. Phases read as they do there (from, to, posture, enemies, declare, ifAtWar, objectives, targets, siege, release, landings, minOdds). 'quiet' means a defence that makes no local counter-attacks because the plan is saving the army for a later stroke. 'withdraw' is for an expedition on an ally's ground: once the enemy holds any place in 'when', its corps make for a port and are taken off. A theatre's 'havens' are where troops lifted off its coasts are taken, the first still in friendly hands with room, rather than all the way home; troops already in a haven stay there. With no phase for the month the army stands on the defensive. As everywhere, the plan only sets intentions: the ground still has to be won.",
